## Contract and architecture audit

The contract checker distinguishes:

- registered canonical names and revisions;
- reviewed unregistered shape owners;
- separate semantic doctrine owners;
- explicitly non-canonical private artifacts;
- pending field-level definition decisions; and
- rejected generic candidates that must not enter `contract_families`.

Registered contracts must carry the registry owner ref, complete contract IDs
and schema versions. Unregistered canonical objects must resolve through a
reviewed exact-token shape owner and may not invent registry identity. Record
families must exactly equal the configured allowlist after safe alias
canonicalization. Pending candidates require a named decision owner and a
literal decision exit, and occur only on proposed records.

The architecture generator reads the current source-of-truth owner table,
contract registry, conformance index, and ADR index rather than trusting the
dated audit alone. A fingerprint forces explicit review when an owner-subject
row changes; current registry and conformance additions become orphans until
mapped. Exact owner fragments and digests, record stages, proposed bounded
owners, contract IDs, conformance refs, product/runtime owners, projection
surfaces, proof definitions, and dispositions are emitted per obligation.

## Hypervisor evidence audit

The retained live artifact is intentionally narrow. It records a supported
local launch and safe transport observations; it neither invokes a mutation nor
uses application controls. The hardened checker rejects a non-loopback base,
an unsupported command, missing/stale source hashes, changed request policy,
missing/reordered routes, request errors, non-2xx/3xx responses, malformed body
metadata, and a false reachability summary.

That bar validates the retained record's internal and source consistency; it
does not make a past server observation cryptographically independent. More
importantly, HTTP reachability and body hashes remain categorically separate
from rendered-surface evidence. The visual-browser failure is therefore an
explicit SKIP with desktop and narrow follow-up obligations, not a degraded
visual PASS.

